{{ja-kanjitab|ご|じゅう|k2=じゅっ|ほ|k3=ぽ|ひゃく|k4=ひゃっ|ほ|k5=ぽ|yomi=on,goon,kanon,goon,kanon|clearright=1}}

===Etymology===
Shortening of {{m|ja|五十歩をもって百歩を笑う|tr=gojippo o motte hyappo o warau||to laugh at a hundred steps after having [gone] fifty steps}}, in turn ultimately a {{lbor|ja|zh|sort=こじっぽひゃっぽ'|nocap=1|五十步笑百步|lit=fifty steps laughs at one hundred steps}}, from a story by [[Mencius]] wherein two characters are running away from a battle and the one going fifty steps laughs at the one having gone one hundred steps for being a coward.  The point of the expression is that both have run away, and the difference is purely a matter of degree.<ref name="KDJ2">{{R:Nihon Kokugo Daijiten 2}}</ref><ref name="DJS">{{R:Daijisen}}</ref><ref name="DJR"/><ref name="SMK5"/>

The longer form is first cited to 1714, and the shortened form is first cited to around 1716.<ref name="KDJ2"/>  The shortened form now appears to have replaced the longer form.

